Transient storage is cleared between external calls, breaking multicall patterns:

Writers: withdraw
Readers: withdraw

❌ This pattern will FAIL in multicall:
multicall([
contract.withdraw(...),  // Sets transient state
contract.withdraw(...)   // State is gone!
]);

Fix 1: Combine into single atomic function
function atomicOperation(uint256 amount) public {
// Set AND use transient state in same call
transientState = amount;
require(transientState > 0);
// ... logic
}

Fix 2: Use persistent storage for multi-call scenarios
uint256 public persistentState;  // NOT transient

Fix 3: Document multicall incompatibility
/// @notice CANNOT be used in multicall - transient storage is cleared
function withdraw() public {
// ...
}

Add explicit cleanup to prevent state pollution:

Bad pattern (state leak):
function process() public {
transientState = msg.value;
// ... logic
// ❌ NO cleanup - pollutes transaction
}

Good pattern (explicit cleanup):
function process() public {
transientState = msg.value;
// ... logic

// ✅ Explicit cleanup
delete transientState;
}

Or use try-finally pattern:
function process() public {
transientState = msg.value;
try this._internalLogic() {
// success
} catch {
// handle error
}
// ✅ Always cleanup
delete transientState;
}

Add read-only reentrancy protection for view functions:

Without EIP-1153, view functions couldn't be exploited during external calls.
With EIP-1153, attackers can set transient state to manipulate view function results.

Add protection:
uint256 transient private locked;

modifier nonReentrant() {
require(locked == 0);
locked = 1;
_;
locked = 0;
}

// ✅ Protect view functions too
function getAccountData(address user) public view returns (uint256, uint256) {
require(locked == 0, "Cannot read during external call");
return (balances[user], debt[user]);
}

This prevents attackers from calling view functions during external calls
to get inconsistent state readings.

